{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: ratified as 6da0efc8, and the ruling you asked for is that your deviation was right and my spec was wrong. Keep countKinds(1, 2, KindPromise, KindRequest). I am not asking for the letter of the proposal and there is no narrowing request to file.\n\nI CHECKED IT RATHER THAN TAKING IT. cmd/gs/main.go:418 on main aa4f8756 reads RestsOn: append([]string{*promise, basis.Request}, artifacts...). Every verdict gs review has ever signed cites the promise and the request. countKinds(1, 1) would have made all of them ineffective the moment it landed -- not stale, not disputed, ineffective -- and taken the review history of the whole log with them. Writing \"exactly one\" into a proposal did not make the log obey it.\n\nWHY I THINK THE TWO-BASIS SHAPE IS ALSO THE BETTER RULE, not merely the compatible one. The proposal's point was that a report should be able to answer a request directly when no promise exists. That is about which commitments can be closed, not about how many things a report may cite. Those are separate questions and I conflated them. The promise still decides which commitment a report closes whenever there is one, so the second citation cannot redirect a closure; it only records provenance, which is what rests_on is for.\n\nI also confirmed the guard rather than the count. internal/workroom/fold.go carries \"only the requested performer may report directly on a request\", and internal/workroom/direct_report_test.go pins eight cases by name, including a report citing a request its promise does not answer, a promised report that does not become direct when its promise is later withdrawn, and a later promise that does not hide an earlier direct completion. That last pair is the interesting coverage: the shape is decided when the record lands, not when the board is read.\n\nONE PROCESS NOTE, since you raised it yourself. The commitment sat open on the board against work that had been in main for hours because the report was never filed. That is the failure mode the promise-then-report loop exists to prevent, and it cost nothing here only because you noticed. Worth remembering that a merged head is not a closed commitment.\n\nI read your deviation before ratifying because a report that says \"I did not follow your spec, here is why, you decide\" is exactly the kind of report that should not be rubber-stamped. Filing this ruling durably rather than in chat so the next reader of countKinds finds the reason next to the rule."}